content:
We asked Melburnians to confess the things they think but never say out loud. The result was a collective therapy session that confirmed what we already suspected: this city has a shared inner monologue, and it&rsquo;s deeply unhinged.
Here are 20 anonymous confessions from people who live here. If you feel personally attacked, that&rsquo;s the point.
The Coffee Confessions 1. &ldquo;I judge people who order cappuccinos after 11am. I know it&rsquo;s irrational. I don&rsquo;t care.&rdquo;
This one came from a barista in Carlton who admitted they physically cringe when someone orders a cap at 2pm. Melbourne has unwritten coffee rules and this is the most rigidly enforced. Nobody knows why. Nobody questions it. We all just comply.
2. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve pretended to like a pour-over when it tasted like hot water that briefly looked at a coffee bean.&rdquo;
The third-wave coffee scene has given us incredible espresso. It has also given us $7 filter coffees that taste like regret. Nobody admits this because the barista spent six minutes explaining the farm elevation and bean varietal before handing you what amounts to expensive dishwater.
3. &ldquo;My favourite coffee in Melbourne comes from a 7-Eleven. I will take this to my grave.&rdquo;
Anonymous for a reason. This person lives in Fitzroy.
The Transport Confessions 4. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve waited 22 minutes for a tram that the app said was 3 minutes away. I didn&rsquo;t move. I refused to let the app win.&rdquo;
PTV Live is the most optimistic piece of software ever written. It treats tram schedules the way horoscopes treat personality — loosely inspired by reality, never actually predictive. Yet we all check it, every single time, like it might suddenly become accurate.
5. &ldquo;I moved to South Yarra specifically for the train line and I still drive everywhere.&rdquo;
The Sandringham line runs every 10 minutes during peak. The station is a 4-minute walk. This person owns a car they can&rsquo;t park and drives it 3km to a shopping centre with paid parking. They know this makes no sense. They do it anyway.
6. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve told interstate visitors the airport train is &lsquo;coming soon&rsquo; for the past 15 years.&rdquo;
It is not coming soon. It may never come. But admitting this feels like admitting defeat, so we keep saying it with slightly less conviction each year. Sydney got theirs in 2000. Melbourne is still talking about alignments.
The Food Confessions 7. &ldquo;I have never been to Lune. I&rsquo;m too scared to admit this in public.&rdquo;
In Melbourne, not having tried Lune is the social equivalent of not having read a book everyone is discussing. You nod along, you say &lsquo;yeah the cruffin is incredible,&rsquo; and you hope nobody asks follow-up questions.
8. &ldquo;I went to Chin Chin once in 2016 and have been telling people it&rsquo;s overrated ever since without going back.&rdquo;
This is peak Melbourne behaviour. Form an opinion on a restaurant eight years ago, never revisit it, and defend that opinion at dinner parties for the rest of your life. Chin Chin has changed chefs, menus, and entire dining concepts since then. Doesn&rsquo;t matter. The opinion is locked in.
9. &ldquo;I tell visitors Lygon Street is a tourist trap but I eat there every second week.&rdquo;
Carlton&rsquo;s Lygon Street gets dismissed by inner-north residents as the place you send your parents when they visit. These same residents are at Papa Gino&rsquo;s on a Thursday night ordering the veal parmigiana. The duality of Melbourne.
The Suburb Confessions 10. &ldquo;I moved to the western suburbs and told everyone it was for &rsquo;the culture.&rsquo; It was for the rent.&rdquo;
Footscray, Yarraville, and Seddon all got the gentrification treatment of being described as &lsquo;up and coming&rsquo; for about 15 years. The actual reason most people moved there was that they couldn&rsquo;t afford Fitzroy anymore. The pho on Footscray&rsquo;s Hopkins Street was a bonus, not the business case.
11. &ldquo;I secretly think Docklands is fine. Not great. But fine.&rdquo;
Saying anything positive about Docklands in Melbourne is social suicide. But this person walks along the waterfront, eats at Cargo, watches the sunset from NewQuay, and thinks it&rsquo;s&hellip; okay. Not every suburb needs to be Fitzroy. Sometimes &lsquo;fine&rsquo; is fine.
12. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve lived in Brunswick for four years and have never been to a gig at the Retreat.&rdquo;
The Retreat Hotel is Brunswick&rsquo;s cultural landmark. It has a massive beer garden, live music every weekend, and a vibe that defines the suburb. This person walks past it every day and goes home to watch Netflix. They feel appropriate shame about this.
13. &ldquo;I told my real estate agent I loved the &lsquo;village feel&rsquo; of my suburb. I moved there because it was the only place I could afford with a parking spot.&rdquo;
Every Melbourne suburb has a &lsquo;village feel&rsquo; if you squint hard enough. What it actually means: there&rsquo;s a strip of shops within walking distance and at least one cafe that does a decent flat white. The &lsquo;village feel&rsquo; language is property marketing that has seeped into how we actually talk about where we live.
The Social Confessions 14. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve RSVPed to a Melbourne event on Facebook with &lsquo;interested&rsquo; and then never gone. Every single time.&rdquo;
Facebook&rsquo;s &lsquo;interested&rsquo; button is Melbourne&rsquo;s polite way of saying &lsquo;I will think about this briefly and then do nothing.&rsquo; It costs nothing, commits to nothing, and lets you feel culturally engaged without leaving the house. We all do it.
15. &ldquo;I judge anyone who calls it &lsquo;Melbs.&rsquo; It&rsquo;s Melbourne. Three syllables. Use them.&rdquo;
This one sparked an argument in our inbox. The &lsquo;Melbs&rsquo; camp says it&rsquo;s affectionate shorthand. The anti-Melbs camp says it sounds like something a Sydney person would say. There is no middle ground.
16. &ldquo;I have told people I &rsquo;love the weather&rsquo; in Melbourne. I was lying. Every time.&rdquo;
Four seasons in one day is not charming. It&rsquo;s exhausting. But admitting you hate Melbourne&rsquo;s weather feels disloyal, so we frame it as &lsquo;character&rsquo; and &lsquo;unpredictability&rsquo; and &lsquo;keeps you on your toes.&rsquo; It doesn&rsquo;t keep you on your toes. It keeps you carrying an umbrella, a jumper, sunglasses, and sunscreen at all times like a paranoid weather prepper.
17. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve gone to a laneway bar and pretended I knew it was there when I actually found it by accident.&rdquo;
Melbourne&rsquo;s laneway culture rewards people who appear to know where they&rsquo;re going. Nobody wants to be the person looking confused on Degraves Street. So you find a bar by stumbling into it, and then you tell friends about it like you&rsquo;ve been going there for years. This is how &lsquo;hidden gems&rsquo; work in this city.
The Lifestyle Confessions 18. &ldquo;I own activewear I have never activated.&rdquo;
Melbourne has more athleisure per capita than any city that isn&rsquo;t LA. The joggers, the Lululemon leggings, the branded hoodies — roughly 60% of this clothing has never been within 500 metres of a gym. It&rsquo;s a uniform. We wear it to brunch.
19. &ldquo;I tell people I go to the Tan every morning. I went twice in January and then the alarm got the better of me.&rdquo;
The Tan Track around the Royal Botanic Gardens is Melbourne&rsquo;s most aspirational exercise route. Telling people you &lsquo;do the Tan&rsquo; implies a level of fitness and discipline that most people achieve for exactly two weeks every New Year before returning to their natural state.
20. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve lived in Melbourne my whole life and still don&rsquo;t understand the hook turn.&rdquo;
Nobody understands the hook turn. Not really. You pull into the intersection, you wait, you hope, and you go. It works because everyone is equally confused and equally cautious. The moment someone actually understands the hook turn with confidence, the system will collapse.
Got a Melbourne confession? Submit it anonymously to hq@melbz.com.au with the subject line #melbzconfessions. The best ones make it into the next round.
